GeneralsX @bugfix Android port 13/09/2026

Stop the ICE STUN request chain from spinning the thinker loop.

Once GetLocalAddresses() started working on Android (see
android-getlocaladdresses.patch), devices began offering every interface they
have, including unroutable ones -- a 29.x placeholder, carrier-NAT 10.x, IPv6
ULAs. A UDP send bound to such a source address fails synchronously, and on
POSIX it fails silently.

That exposed a latent defect. CSteamNetworkingSocketsSTUNRequest::Think treats
an immediate send failure as terminal: it invokes the callback and deletes
itself on the first Think, never reaching the retry schedule. The callback,
STUNRequestCallback_ServerReflexiveCandidate, responds by trying "the next
STUN server" -- index_of(addr) + 1. But index_of returns the FIRST match, and
ResolveHostname produces runs of identical addresses, so the next server is
the same server. The new request is queued with SetNextThinkTimeASAP(), an
absolute timestamp of 1 microsecond, which is permanently in the past.

So each iteration creates one object, fails to send, and queues a successor
that is already overdue. Thinker_ProcessThinkers runs to its 10000 iteration
cap, holding the global lock throughout, and then does it again: a device log
shows 4715 such passes over 82 seconds, with the game's main thread waiting up
to 24.8 seconds for that lock. To the player the game is simply frozen, with
no sound, until they background it.

The duplicates come from two places at once: getaddrinfo is called with
ai_socktype 0, which returns each address once per socket type, and the five
Google STUN hostnames in the configured list all resolve to one IP.

De-duplicating the resolved lists breaks the fixed point, and the walk ends by
falling off the end of the list as it was meant to. The TURN list is built the
same way and feeds an identical index_of(addr)+1 chain in
STUNRequestCallback_AllocateRelay, so it gets the same treatment, skipping the
matching credential so the two vectors stay parallel.

The keepalive callback is a third instance and worse: it cycles with % len(),
so it has no termination condition even with a perfectly unique list. It no
longer re-queues; Think_KeepAliveOnCandidates already retries on its own timer.

This is not Android-specific. Any platform using Valve's native ICE
implementation -- ENABLE_ICE without USE_STEAMWEBRTC, which is our
configuration and not the one Steam ships -- can hit it with a single
unroutable local address.

